WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.494
- You know, courage without genius

00:00:01.494 --> 00:00:02.820
might not get you where you need to go,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.683
but genius without courage
almost certainly won't.

00:00:04.683 --> 00:00:06.098
In this industry, you
don't have a business

00:00:06.098 --> 00:00:07.203
until you have a product.

00:00:07.203 --> 00:00:09.122
The entrepreneur with the
idea at the right time

00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:10.292
is going to look like a genius.

00:00:10.292 --> 00:00:11.609
The entrepreneur with the
right idea at the wrong time

00:00:11.609 --> 00:00:12.643
is going to look like a moron.

00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:13.601
Failure is a wonderful thing.

00:00:13.601 --> 00:00:14.684
Failure teaches you all this stuff,

00:00:14.684 --> 00:00:16.112
and it's great to fail a lot.

00:00:16.112 --> 00:00:17.440
Like, and we don't buy any of that.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.213
New companies generally shouldn't exist.

00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:22.618
Existing companies are usually
pretty good at what they do.

00:00:22.618 --> 00:00:24.244
Having the right idea and
having it at the right time

00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:27.145
being able to execute on it is 99% of it.

00:00:27.145 --> 00:00:28.263
No matter how dire it gets,

00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:30.251
no matter how like evil Moriarty is,

00:00:30.251 --> 00:00:31.632
Sherlock's going to have
that stroke of genius

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:32.514
that's going to save the day.

00:00:32.514 --> 00:00:36.462
I think that the development,
career path, skills training

00:00:36.462 --> 00:00:37.877
is going to be such a
more fundamental part

00:00:37.877 --> 00:00:38.962
of how the economy works.

00:00:38.962 --> 00:00:41.416
Me and my business partner at
my last company, Ben Horowitz,

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:44.896
coined a term he calls the,
"Law of Crappy People."

00:00:44.896 --> 00:00:46.908
You've been sitting here
listening to me for 20 minutes,

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:48.490
and I've been working
on this for five years,

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.418
and you think you understand this so well

00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:52.236
that you can make me a suggestion.

00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:54.527
- He's an American entrepreneur,
venture capitalist,

00:00:54.527 --> 00:00:55.875
and software engineer.

00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:57.598
He's the co-founder and general partner

00:00:57.598 --> 00:01:00.166
of the Silicon Valley venture capital firm

00:01:00.166 --> 00:01:01.478
Andreessen Horowitz.

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:04.626
He has an estimated net
worth of 600 million dollars.

00:01:04.626 --> 00:01:05.737
He's Mark Andreessen,

00:01:05.737 --> 00:01:08.070
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.073
(loud bang)

00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:12.590
- The two things we really zero in on

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.680
in people are two things.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.838
They sound simple, they
not being very difficult

00:01:16.838 --> 00:01:19.218
courage and genius.

00:01:19.218 --> 00:01:20.357
Courage is the one we talk about a lot,

00:01:20.357 --> 00:01:23.142
because it's the one people can learn.

00:01:23.142 --> 00:01:24.829
Courage, which is to say

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:27.132
not giving up in the face of adversity,

00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:29.400
just being absolutely
determined to succeed,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.851
is something that you
can force yourself to do.

00:01:31.851 --> 00:01:32.684
It can be very painful.

00:01:32.684 --> 00:01:33.929
You can force yourself to do it.

00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:35.199
The genius part is a little bit harder

00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:37.666
to force yourself to do.

00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:39.202
Courage without genius might not get you

00:01:39.202 --> 00:01:40.160
where you need to go,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.401
but genius without courage
almost certainly won't.

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:44.336
And so, we're looking for
some kind of magic combination

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:45.636
of genius and courage.

00:01:45.636 --> 00:01:46.976
We don't believe there's a tension

00:01:46.976 --> 00:01:47.865
between product and business.

00:01:47.865 --> 00:01:49.291
We believe there's a staging.

00:01:49.291 --> 00:01:51.128
Which is, in this industry,
you don't have a business

00:01:51.128 --> 00:01:52.556
until you have a product.

00:01:52.556 --> 00:01:53.760
And in particular, you
don't have a business

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.060
until you have a product that
a lot of people will want.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.383
We have this concept, we use
the term, "product/market fit",

00:01:58.383 --> 00:02:00.669
which is basically you have a
product that the market wants,

00:02:00.669 --> 00:02:02.916
and you can tell because the
market's pulling the product.

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:06.407
And until you have that, time
spent building the business

00:02:06.407 --> 00:02:08.203
around the product is pointless,

00:02:08.203 --> 00:02:09.786
because it's not going to go anywhere.

00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:11.652
Like, best case, it's
going to be a zombie.

00:02:11.652 --> 00:02:12.593
There's a lot of companies,

00:02:12.593 --> 00:02:13.426
or in the Valley,

00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:14.486
there's a lot of companies
in the Valley and elsewhere

00:02:14.486 --> 00:02:16.556
that are 25, 50, 75 people.

00:02:16.556 --> 00:02:18.054
They've got a product,
nobody really wants it.

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:19.405
They sell a few every quarter,

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:20.754
and they kind of eke out a living.

00:02:20.754 --> 00:02:23.957
And they go on for year after
year after year after year

00:02:23.957 --> 00:02:25.165
after year and they never get anywhere.

00:02:25.165 --> 00:02:26.556
They never go public.

00:02:26.556 --> 00:02:27.856
They never get bought,

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.436
and people just basically
collect salaries.

00:02:29.436 --> 00:02:32.799
And that's not what we
want to do, and that's not

00:02:32.799 --> 00:02:35.771
what super high potential
entrepreneurs want to do.

00:02:35.771 --> 00:02:36.974
So, what we basically say is,

00:02:36.974 --> 00:02:37.933
if you want to build a big success,

00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:39.206
you've got to nail the product.

00:02:39.206 --> 00:02:40.938
It's got to be a killer product.

00:02:40.938 --> 00:02:43.041
It's got to be something
that has product/market fit.

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:44.504
When you have product/market fit,

00:02:44.504 --> 00:02:46.528
then you build a company around it, right?

00:02:46.528 --> 00:02:47.730
So then you build a sales force,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.689
then you start doing marketing,

00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:49.923
then you start doing business deals,

00:02:49.923 --> 00:02:51.415
then you start expanding internationally,

00:02:51.415 --> 00:02:52.698
and you do all this other stuff.

00:02:52.698 --> 00:02:54.422
And it's at that point
where you start to apply

00:02:54.422 --> 00:02:56.123
a lot of capital, and
you build a big board,

00:02:56.123 --> 00:02:57.885
and you start hiring executives
and doing all this stuff.

00:02:57.885 --> 00:03:00.173
But a full executive
team, with a sales force,

00:03:00.173 --> 00:03:01.751
all that stuff before
you have a killer product

00:03:01.751 --> 00:03:03.158
is a complete waste of time.

00:03:03.158 --> 00:03:05.060
Entrepreneurs are often uniquely bad

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.060
at wrapping their heads around timing,

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.146
'cause entrepreneurs by definition

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:11.245
are highly optimistic people,
'cause you have to be.

00:03:11.245 --> 00:03:13.099
'Cause you're starting a
company, and you have to be

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:16.086
highly optimistic if you're
going to set off and do that.

00:03:16.086 --> 00:03:17.596
And you're probably so optimistic,

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:19.478
that you're probably too
aggressive on timing,

00:03:19.478 --> 00:03:21.499
and you probably assume that
something is going to happen

00:03:21.499 --> 00:03:23.485
in the market, faster than
it's actually going to.

00:03:23.485 --> 00:03:25.444
And so, sometimes what you see happen,

00:03:25.444 --> 00:03:28.131
you see the Friendster,
Myspace, and Facebook scenario.

00:03:28.131 --> 00:03:29.680
You see that happen a lot.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.308
You see a company fail,
and then another company

00:03:32.308 --> 00:03:34.377
with the exact same idea six years later,

00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:36.431
and that's a huge success.

00:03:36.431 --> 00:03:37.856
And it's just flat out, simply timing

00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:39.215
and I don't know what to say,

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:40.591
well I don't know in general

00:03:40.591 --> 00:03:42.240
what to say about that in the specifics,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.015
there's a lot of specific
things I think you do

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.046
to try and address that.

00:03:45.046 --> 00:03:45.879
You try to keep your

00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:46.712
(mumbles)
rate low.

00:03:46.712 --> 00:03:49.362
You try to give yourself runway
for the market to develop.

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:50.857
You try to correct for the fact

00:03:50.857 --> 00:03:52.059
that you're probably being too optimistic.

00:03:52.059 --> 00:03:54.413
But, I think in practice,
that's a lot of luck.

00:03:54.413 --> 00:03:56.854
The entrepreneur with the
idea at the right time

00:03:56.854 --> 00:03:57.812
is going to look like a genius.

00:03:57.812 --> 00:03:58.903
The entrepreneur with the
right idea at the wrong time

00:03:58.903 --> 00:04:00.466
is going to look like a moron.

00:04:00.466 --> 00:04:01.694
And it's really hard for an entrepreneur

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:02.527
to tell the difference.

00:04:02.527 --> 00:04:04.858
And by the way, there's
always been this kind of thing

00:04:04.858 --> 00:04:08.237
in Silicon Valley, of this sort
of, "failure fetish," right?

00:04:08.237 --> 00:04:09.195
Failure is good, right?

00:04:09.195 --> 00:04:11.878
Failure is, all you guys were
probably all been taught this,

00:04:11.878 --> 00:04:12.976
heard about this from a lot of people.

00:04:12.976 --> 00:04:13.955
Failure is a wonderful thing.

00:04:13.955 --> 00:04:15.113
Failure teaches you all this stuff,

00:04:15.113 --> 00:04:18.661
and it's great to fail a lot,
and we don't buy any of that.

00:04:18.661 --> 00:04:20.848
We think that's all
complete, complete nonsense.

00:04:20.848 --> 00:04:22.427
We think failure sucks.

00:04:22.427 --> 00:04:24.635
(audience laughing)

00:04:24.635 --> 00:04:25.930
We think failure is a terribly,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.733
terribly depressing thing to go through.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.872
We think success on the
other hand is wonderful.

00:04:29.872 --> 00:04:31.389
(audience laughing)

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:33.161
You know, you wouldn't
think that this is something

00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:34.262
you have to actually say out loud,

00:04:34.262 --> 00:04:36.535
but we do find it to be clarifying

00:04:36.535 --> 00:04:37.618
when we point it out.

00:04:37.618 --> 00:04:39.165
(audience laughing)

00:04:39.165 --> 00:04:41.478
And so we are strongly
biased towards people

00:04:41.478 --> 00:04:43.040
who are so determined to succeed

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.505
that they just never
give up, they never quit.

00:04:45.505 --> 00:04:46.902
And I think that is a huge part of it.

00:04:46.902 --> 00:04:49.798
And that's something we
try to look deeply for.

00:04:49.798 --> 00:04:52.084
That's the kind of thing that's
not listed on a resume, right?

00:04:52.084 --> 00:04:53.898
That's something that has
to be deep in somebody's,

00:04:53.898 --> 00:04:55.410
fundamentally deep in
somebody's character,

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.043
and you have to see it
in their backgrounds.

00:04:57.043 --> 00:04:59.668
New companies generally shouldn't exist.

00:04:59.668 --> 00:05:02.911
Existing companies are usually
pretty good at what they do.

00:05:02.911 --> 00:05:06.504
And so, for a new company
to exist, it not only has to

00:05:06.504 --> 00:05:08.571
come in and go into business,
and bring a product market,

00:05:08.571 --> 00:05:10.639
but it has to bring a product
market that's so much better

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:12.831
than what already exists,
that it punches through

00:05:12.831 --> 00:05:14.360
the sort of status quo.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.714
Most customers in most
markets are pretty happy

00:05:16.714 --> 00:05:17.977
buying from the current suppliers,

00:05:17.977 --> 00:05:20.495
so there has to be a
real edge on the thing.

00:05:20.495 --> 00:05:22.032
And we look for that in
either a technology change,

00:05:22.032 --> 00:05:25.152
usually a technology change
or an economic change,

00:05:25.152 --> 00:05:26.334
which are often the same thing.

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:28.083
The single best piece of
advice I've ever heard,

00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:29.041
I heard the other day

00:05:29.041 --> 00:05:32.269
watching Steve Martin, the
comedian, on Charlie Rose.

00:05:32.269 --> 00:05:33.906
Steve Martin, that I'm
sure everybody in the room

00:05:33.906 --> 00:05:35.647
knows who Steve Martin is, but he's one of

00:05:35.647 --> 00:05:39.071
the most successful comedians
in the last 30 years

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:40.746
across many different media,

00:05:40.746 --> 00:05:44.201
across television, movies,
and books, and everything.

00:05:44.201 --> 00:05:45.810
He's been tremendously successful.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.527
He recently wrote a book about,

00:05:47.527 --> 00:05:48.519
it's actually really funny

00:05:48.519 --> 00:05:51.636
'cause it's a book about
how he became successful,

00:05:51.636 --> 00:05:53.776
and it's not even a funny book.

00:05:53.776 --> 00:05:55.383
I mean, it's Steve Martin,
so it's a little bit funny,

00:05:55.383 --> 00:05:56.693
but it's not a comedy book.

00:05:56.693 --> 00:06:00.256
He actually sat down and
wrote a book of how I did it.

00:06:00.256 --> 00:06:01.579
And in particular, right in comedy,

00:06:01.579 --> 00:06:03.186
comedy is a little bit like startups,

00:06:03.186 --> 00:06:04.772
in that there are a few
comedians like Jerry Seinfeld,

00:06:04.772 --> 00:06:06.621
or Steve Martin, that
are hugely successful.

00:06:06.621 --> 00:06:08.215
And then there's like,
for every one of those,

00:06:08.215 --> 00:06:10.603
there's 10 thousand others who
are still playing nightclubs

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:13.322
in very, very small towns.

00:06:13.322 --> 00:06:16.107
So there's a huge dispersion
from the big winners

00:06:16.107 --> 00:06:20.064
to a very large number of
people who work equally hard,

00:06:20.064 --> 00:06:22.203
who don't get there.

00:06:22.203 --> 00:06:24.262
And he boiled it down to
something that I think

00:06:24.262 --> 00:06:26.158
was incredibly insightful.

00:06:26.158 --> 00:06:27.961
Obvious, but insightful.

00:06:27.961 --> 00:06:31.328
He said, "Young comedians
always come to me and they say,

00:06:31.328 --> 00:06:32.379
"How do I be like you?

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:33.472
"How do I be successful like you?"

00:06:33.472 --> 00:06:35.381
And he said they're
always looking for like,

00:06:35.381 --> 00:06:36.272
"How do I get an agent?"

00:06:36.272 --> 00:06:37.133
Or "How do I get booked?"

00:06:37.133 --> 00:06:38.478
Or "How do I get on the Tonight Show?"

00:06:38.478 --> 00:06:40.459
Or "How do I get a movie role?"

00:06:40.459 --> 00:06:42.276
And he said, the real answer is

00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:45.579
be so good that they can't ignore you.

00:06:45.579 --> 00:06:48.874
And it turns out that he just,

00:06:48.874 --> 00:06:50.057
and this is what his book talks about,

00:06:50.057 --> 00:06:53.077
is he applied himself
with a level of discipline

00:06:53.077 --> 00:06:54.695
at being an amazing comedian.

00:06:54.695 --> 00:06:55.528
And it's really funny, right?

00:06:55.528 --> 00:06:56.954
Steve Martin's a great
illustration of this,

00:06:56.954 --> 00:06:59.864
'cause Steve Martin on
stage is incredibly silly,

00:06:59.864 --> 00:07:01.420
and he's got the banjo,
and he's got the arrow.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.905
If you watch his footage from the 70s

00:07:02.905 --> 00:07:05.037
when he was up-and-coming.

00:07:05.037 --> 00:07:07.370
He's got the arrow through
his head, he's dancing around,

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:09.521
he's doing all this crazy stuff,

00:07:09.521 --> 00:07:11.538
and it looks like he's just clowning.

00:07:11.538 --> 00:07:13.863
But the level of discipline and rigor

00:07:13.863 --> 00:07:16.231
and practice that he put into it

00:07:16.231 --> 00:07:18.790
was astonishing over a decade long period.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.322
And then after a decade of hard work

00:07:20.322 --> 00:07:22.741
was a sudden overnight success.

00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:24.812
The key point there being,

00:07:24.812 --> 00:07:27.550
be so good that they can't ignore you.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.754
If there's a forum like
this, and a group like this,

00:07:30.754 --> 00:07:34.168
and efforts like this, and my blog,

00:07:34.168 --> 00:07:35.636
and all these things.

00:07:35.636 --> 00:07:38.824
We all talk about the mechanics
of how do you hire people,

00:07:38.824 --> 00:07:40.978
or how do you raise money,
or how do you do this,

00:07:40.978 --> 00:07:43.487
or how do you do that, or
how do you sell your company?

00:07:43.487 --> 00:07:45.529
And a lot of that, it's important stuff,

00:07:45.529 --> 00:07:46.840
but a lot of it is when to addressing

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.069
around the core proposition
of the core problem,

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.753
which is how to be so good
that they can't ignore you.

00:07:51.753 --> 00:07:53.837
And in a startup, a lot
of that has to do with

00:07:53.837 --> 00:07:57.106
have an idea that's so good
that they can't ignore you.

00:07:57.106 --> 00:07:59.168
And the best idea,

00:07:59.168 --> 00:08:01.363
having the right idea and
having it at the right time

00:08:01.363 --> 00:08:04.495
and being able to execute
on it is 99% of it,

00:08:04.495 --> 00:08:06.736
and the rest of the stuff
that we all talk about,

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:08.120
that I talk about all the time,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.597
is all add-on and ancillary to that.

00:08:10.597 --> 00:08:11.726
There are no silver bullets.

00:08:11.726 --> 00:08:14.052
There are only lead bullets.

00:08:14.052 --> 00:08:15.663
There's this temptation,

00:08:15.663 --> 00:08:16.995
especially when you get into crisis,

00:08:16.995 --> 00:08:18.491
when you get into a real problem.

00:08:18.491 --> 00:08:19.415
There's this temptation to think,

00:08:19.415 --> 00:08:20.578
"There must be a magic answer.

00:08:20.578 --> 00:08:22.708
"There must be some stroke of genius."

00:08:22.708 --> 00:08:23.983
It's almost, it's like,

00:08:23.983 --> 00:08:25.198
I don't know if people watch

00:08:25.198 --> 00:08:27.372
the new Sherlock Holmes TV
series, which I just love.

00:08:27.372 --> 00:08:29.038
It's like, you know,
Sherlock is going to have,

00:08:29.038 --> 00:08:30.182
no matter how dire it gets,

00:08:30.182 --> 00:08:31.808
no matter how evil Moriarty is,

00:08:31.808 --> 00:08:33.390
Sherlock's going to have
that stroke of genius

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:34.294
that's going to save the day.

00:08:34.294 --> 00:08:35.989
And there's this really strong tendency

00:08:35.989 --> 00:08:38.937
to kind of think that that's out there.

00:08:38.937 --> 00:08:40.132
And we see a lot of entrepreneurs

00:08:40.132 --> 00:08:41.975
that cycle through
different silver bullets,

00:08:41.975 --> 00:08:43.204
and then they don't work,
and they don't work,

00:08:43.204 --> 00:08:44.687
and they don't work.

00:08:44.687 --> 00:08:46.768
Ben's point is always,
the answer's probably

00:08:46.768 --> 00:08:48.517
in firing a whole bunch of lead bullets.

00:08:48.517 --> 00:08:49.955
So the answer probably is

00:08:49.955 --> 00:08:53.015
the engineers working later
at night for six months,

00:08:53.015 --> 00:08:55.397
and getting the next
version of the product out.

00:08:55.397 --> 00:08:57.563
And the answer is probably
for the sales reps

00:08:57.563 --> 00:09:00.255
to go call on twice as many
customers and close more deals.

00:09:00.255 --> 00:09:01.569
And the answer is probably to, you know,

00:09:01.569 --> 00:09:03.851
your stock price is low,
go find the investors

00:09:03.851 --> 00:09:04.809
who are willing to invest

00:09:04.809 --> 00:09:06.348
when you're trading in half the cash.

00:09:06.348 --> 00:09:07.856
'Cause it turns out
they actually do exist,

00:09:07.856 --> 00:09:09.065
and then your stock goes up a little bit,

00:09:09.065 --> 00:09:11.602
and people start to regain confidence.

00:09:11.602 --> 00:09:14.056
So I would say I've certainly come around

00:09:14.056 --> 00:09:14.889
to that point of view a lot,

00:09:14.889 --> 00:09:16.716
and so when we work with entrepreneurs,

00:09:16.716 --> 00:09:19.123
we often have very similar
advice, 'cause it's kind of,

00:09:19.123 --> 00:09:20.830
it'll be tempered through
the very practical realities

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.233
of what you have to do to get
through a situation like that.

00:09:23.233 --> 00:09:25.517
I think our companies
systematically are too weak

00:09:25.517 --> 00:09:27.428
on helping people evolve in their careers

00:09:27.428 --> 00:09:28.535
and develop new skills.

00:09:28.535 --> 00:09:29.676
And I think that same thing is true

00:09:29.676 --> 00:09:30.866
across the entire economy.

00:09:30.866 --> 00:09:33.007
And you almost can't be too good at it.

00:09:33.007 --> 00:09:34.762
The classic example in
the Valley right now,

00:09:34.762 --> 00:09:37.294
is everybody needs mobile,
everybody needs iOS programmers.

00:09:37.294 --> 00:09:38.900
So everybody's out trying
to recruit the same,

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.027
it feels like there's
only 3,000 iOS programmers

00:09:41.027 --> 00:09:43.014
in the Valley, and everybody's
trying to recruit them all.

00:09:43.014 --> 00:09:45.228
It's like, well you've got,

00:09:45.228 --> 00:09:46.882
in a lot of these companies,
you've got Java programmers,

00:09:46.882 --> 00:09:48.107
and you've got PHP programmers,

00:09:48.107 --> 00:09:49.384
and you've got Web programmers.

00:09:49.384 --> 00:09:51.216
It's like, take four months,

00:09:51.216 --> 00:09:52.799
and we actually,

00:09:52.799 --> 00:09:54.631
there's a training company we work with

00:09:54.631 --> 00:09:56.464
called Big Nerd Ranch.

00:09:57.706 --> 00:09:58.539
They're spectacular.

00:09:58.539 --> 00:10:00.542
They literally are, it's Big Nerd Ranch.

00:10:00.542 --> 00:10:01.699
It's where you send people off,

00:10:01.699 --> 00:10:02.532
and they train them
(man laughing)

00:10:02.532 --> 00:10:03.823
on how to be iOS programmers

00:10:03.823 --> 00:10:06.372
at the Big Nerd Ranch.

00:10:06.372 --> 00:10:07.451
- [Man] Dear God.

00:10:07.451 --> 00:10:09.098
- Yeah, everybody's writing that down.

00:10:09.098 --> 00:10:10.393
(audience laughing)

00:10:10.393 --> 00:10:12.529
Or he's pointing to
somebody that's been there.

00:10:12.529 --> 00:10:15.958
And so, train people up
on how to do these things.

00:10:15.958 --> 00:10:17.557
We have another company called the Udacity

00:10:17.557 --> 00:10:19.235
that does this kind of
training now online,

00:10:19.235 --> 00:10:20.358
and is rolling out all these tracks

00:10:20.358 --> 00:10:21.949
to be able to train people
how to do mobile development,

00:10:21.949 --> 00:10:25.150
or how to train people
how to do data science.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:29.511
And so I think the development,
career path, skills training

00:10:29.511 --> 00:10:31.184
is going to be such a
more fundamental part

00:10:31.184 --> 00:10:33.844
of how the economy works,
and I think it has to be.

00:10:33.844 --> 00:10:36.092
Because today we're all
hiring mobile developers.

00:10:36.092 --> 00:10:37.777
Three years from now we're
all going to need to hire

00:10:37.777 --> 00:10:39.742
neural implant developers or
whatever it's going to be.

00:10:39.742 --> 00:10:40.575
- [Man] I love it.

00:10:40.575 --> 00:10:42.619
- And we're going to have
that exact same problem again.

00:10:42.619 --> 00:10:45.144
- How do you as you grow the
startup to be a lot bigger

00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:47.600
prevent mediocrity from creeping in.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.711
- So I love, this question I love

00:10:49.711 --> 00:10:52.439
because I think there's actually,

00:10:52.439 --> 00:10:55.712
a way to think of this is
from a systems standpoint.

00:10:55.712 --> 00:10:59.483
So, my business partner at my
last company, Ben Horowitz,

00:10:59.483 --> 00:11:03.650
coined a term he calls,
"The Law of Crappy People."

00:11:05.296 --> 00:11:06.810
We focused on it a lot at my last company

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.730
and focus on it a lot
in my current company.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:11.446
Basically, the Law of Crappy People,

00:11:11.446 --> 00:11:13.154
is basically a derivation
of the concept that

00:11:13.154 --> 00:11:15.367
A people hire A people,
and B people hire C people,

00:11:15.367 --> 00:11:17.793
which is kind of a cliche and
everybody kind of knows that.

00:11:17.793 --> 00:11:21.006
But the Law of Crappy
People kicks in as you grow.

00:11:21.006 --> 00:11:22.919
And what the "Law of
Crappy People" really is

00:11:22.919 --> 00:11:26.583
is that the capabilities or the competence

00:11:26.583 --> 00:11:30.967
of the people at any level
in your company as you grow,

00:11:30.967 --> 00:11:33.683
are going to degrade to the worst person

00:11:33.683 --> 00:11:35.997
at that level in your company.

00:11:35.997 --> 00:11:38.414
And so, your vice presidents,

00:11:39.455 --> 00:11:42.165
over time, are all going to be as bad

00:11:42.165 --> 00:11:44.106
as your worst vice president.

00:11:44.106 --> 00:11:45.643
Your directors over
time, are all going to be

00:11:45.643 --> 00:11:47.561
as bad as your worst director.

00:11:47.561 --> 00:11:49.531
Your engineers over
time, are all going to be

00:11:49.531 --> 00:11:51.342
as bad as your worst engineer.

00:11:51.342 --> 00:11:53.285
And the reason is because the way

00:11:53.285 --> 00:11:54.729
that hiring decisions get made,

00:11:54.729 --> 00:11:56.370
and the way that promotion
decisions get made

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:58.064
inside a company, is within reference

00:11:58.064 --> 00:11:59.839
to the people who are already there.

00:11:59.839 --> 00:12:01.081
So, I'm a manager.

00:12:01.081 --> 00:12:03.555
I want Joe to get promoted from engineer

00:12:03.555 --> 00:12:05.138
to senior engineer.

00:12:06.303 --> 00:12:08.056
My argument is he's a
really good engineer,

00:12:08.056 --> 00:12:09.927
and the other executives who are battling

00:12:09.927 --> 00:12:11.331
for promotions for their people say,

00:12:11.331 --> 00:12:12.289
"Well, he's not that good."

00:12:12.289 --> 00:12:15.606
And then I say, "Well, he's at
least as good you know, Bob,

00:12:15.606 --> 00:12:18.964
"who is your guy, who
is a senior engineer."

00:12:18.964 --> 00:12:21.955
And you're like, okay,
yeah I guess that's true.

00:12:21.955 --> 00:12:24.003
Bob isn't that great, and
Bob is a senior engineer,

00:12:24.003 --> 00:12:26.600
so clearly Joe should
also be a senior engineer.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.810
And before you know it,
all your senior engineers

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.322
are as bad as Bob.

00:12:30.322 --> 00:12:33.563
And it's absolutely astonishing

00:12:33.563 --> 00:12:34.979
it's absolutely astonishing to watch

00:12:34.979 --> 00:12:36.223
because it basically degrades

00:12:36.223 --> 00:12:38.031
if somebody significantly
better than the worst person,

00:12:38.031 --> 00:12:39.566
they get promoted.

00:12:39.566 --> 00:12:40.723
And when new people get hired,

00:12:40.723 --> 00:12:42.193
they get hired in reference to

00:12:42.193 --> 00:12:43.071
the worst person at the level.

00:12:43.071 --> 00:12:47.110
So, it's basically on
a level by level basis.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:50.391
And you basically need to just
beat it back with a stick.

00:12:50.391 --> 00:12:53.792
You need to be so
determined to not have the,

00:12:53.792 --> 00:12:56.063
another term that people use,

00:12:56.063 --> 00:12:58.661
"The soft bigotry of low expectations."

00:12:58.661 --> 00:13:00.424
You need to be absolutely
determined to have

00:13:00.424 --> 00:13:03.784
your definition of good not be the average

00:13:03.784 --> 00:13:05.255
of the people we have in the company

00:13:05.255 --> 00:13:07.630
or the person who was
accidentally promoted

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:09.744
to a certain level who
really shouldn't have been,

00:13:09.744 --> 00:13:11.870
but now that he is, that's
the definition of the level,

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:13.728
'cause he's now a part of it.

00:13:13.728 --> 00:13:16.116
And you just need to absolutely
beat it back with a stick.

00:13:16.116 --> 00:13:17.578
And one of the heuristics
I think you can use

00:13:17.578 --> 00:13:20.503
to beat it back is to
ask yourself a question,

00:13:20.503 --> 00:13:23.041
on every hire and on every promotion is

00:13:23.041 --> 00:13:25.415
"Are you raising the average?"

00:13:25.415 --> 00:13:26.781
Because you're probably either,

00:13:26.781 --> 00:13:28.897
if you're hiring somebody
or promoting somebody,

00:13:28.897 --> 00:13:32.210
you're either going to be raising
the average at that level,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:33.802
or for that role in the company,

00:13:33.802 --> 00:13:35.608
or you're going to be
lowering the average.

00:13:35.608 --> 00:13:37.577
And if you're continuously
raising the average,

00:13:37.577 --> 00:13:38.550
which is very hard to do,

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:41.163
then you're going to defeat
the Law of Crappy People.

00:13:41.163 --> 00:13:42.486
But if you're not super focused

00:13:42.486 --> 00:13:43.444
and super determined on that,

00:13:43.444 --> 00:13:45.264
then it will inevitably degrade.

00:13:45.264 --> 00:13:47.351
Really bright founders, with
these really radical ideas

00:13:47.351 --> 00:13:49.132
tend to go through what
they call the "idea maze".

00:13:49.132 --> 00:13:51.200
So, they tend to have worked for years,

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.658
working their way through the idea

00:13:52.658 --> 00:13:54.458
to try and figure out how to get from

00:13:54.458 --> 00:13:56.466
the initial crazy starting
point, to at the end

00:13:56.466 --> 00:13:58.640
something that will actually
work in the real world.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:13:59.966
And the really great entrepreneurs

00:13:59.966 --> 00:14:01.141
can walk you through the "idea maze",

00:14:01.141 --> 00:14:03.066
and make you understand
the flow of thinking

00:14:03.066 --> 00:14:05.024
that got them to the point where

00:14:05.024 --> 00:14:05.988
they actually came out the other end

00:14:05.988 --> 00:14:08.552
with what is a great idea.

00:14:08.552 --> 00:14:10.999
And what's interesting about that is

00:14:10.999 --> 00:14:12.710
those are generally not,

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:14.979
there's this kind of a
theory in venture capital

00:14:14.979 --> 00:14:17.213
you want to back coachable entrepreneurs.

00:14:17.213 --> 00:14:19.422
The entrepreneurs who really
have the radical ideas,

00:14:19.422 --> 00:14:21.359
are generally not only not coachable,

00:14:21.359 --> 00:14:24.392
they generally react with
hostility to being coached.

00:14:24.392 --> 00:14:27.358
So one of the things we test for is,

00:14:27.358 --> 00:14:28.457
we basically say, "Well, you know,

00:14:28.457 --> 00:14:31.079
"have you thought about
doing it this other way?"

00:14:31.079 --> 00:14:32.067
What we're not looking for is the,

00:14:32.067 --> 00:14:33.632
"Oh, that's a great idea."

00:14:33.632 --> 00:14:35.697
What we are looking for is
the stare that's just like,

00:14:35.697 --> 00:14:36.746
"You idiots."

00:14:36.746 --> 00:14:37.779
(audience laughing)
Right?

00:14:37.779 --> 00:14:38.712
"You moron.

00:14:38.712 --> 00:14:39.854
"You've been sitting here listening ... "

00:14:39.854 --> 00:14:41.045
And you know, this is them talking to me.

00:14:41.045 --> 00:14:42.808
"You've been sitting here
listening to me for 20 minutes,

00:14:42.808 --> 00:14:44.483
"and I've been working
on this for five years,

00:14:44.483 --> 00:14:46.139
"and you think you understand this so well

00:14:46.139 --> 00:14:47.731
"that you can make me a suggestion?

00:14:47.731 --> 00:14:49.673
"And not only are you an idiot
for thinking you can do that,

00:14:49.673 --> 00:14:51.412
"but I will now explain to you, in detail,

00:14:51.412 --> 00:14:53.236
"why you're that big of an idiot."

00:14:53.236 --> 00:14:54.069
We love those.

00:14:54.069 --> 00:14:55.578
(audience laughing)

00:14:55.578 --> 00:14:56.493
Those are fantastic.

00:14:56.493 --> 00:14:58.378
Those are outstanding.

00:14:58.378 --> 00:15:00.537
And it goes right back to the combination

00:15:00.537 --> 00:15:03.414
of genius and courage I was talking about.

00:15:03.414 --> 00:15:05.056
So in particular, part
of it's in the words,

00:15:05.056 --> 00:15:07.163
but also part of it's
just the look on the face.

00:15:07.163 --> 00:15:08.341
We love that look.

00:15:08.341 --> 00:15:09.282
(audience laughing)

00:15:09.282 --> 00:15:10.284
It's like caviar.

00:15:10.284 --> 00:15:11.880
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.385
I made this video because FBGM 247

00:15:15.385 --> 00:15:16.451
asked me to.

00:15:16.451 --> 00:15:17.641
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:15:17.641 --> 00:15:19.557
that you want me to profile next

00:15:19.557 --> 00:15:22.135
leave it in the comments below,
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:22.135 --> 00:15:24.487
I'd also love to know which
of Marc's top ten rules

00:15:24.487 --> 00:15:25.455
hit you the hardest,

00:15:25.455 --> 00:15:28.142
is going to have the biggest
impact on you business and why.

00:15:28.142 --> 00:15:29.010
Leave it in the comments,

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.196
I'll join in the discussion.

00:15:30.196 --> 00:15:32.597
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:15:32.597 --> 00:15:34.536
and I'll see you soon.

00:15:34.536 --> 00:15:36.869
